The routine sin-m computes the sine of a whole number of degrees, from a table every 5 degrees.

Result: 1000 sin x rounded to nearest, for x a whole number of degrees from 0 to 359, held to -999..999. A cell
holds three digits, so STO would keep 1000 as 000; the result is capped at 999 instead, which makes sin 89, 90
and 91 all 999 and sin 269, 270 and 271 all -999.

Call: load x into the accumulator and JMP 27. It returns with the result in the accumulator.
Cells 27-98 (72 cells). It writes cell 99 and, of its own cells, 59-60, 62, 66-67, 89, 98; nothing else.
Cost: at most 113 instructions a call, 74.5 on average over the domain, counted from the calling JMP to the return
jump. Worst error: 1 in the last digit (thousandths) against the correctly rounded value, at x = 1; 88 of the 360
inputs are off.
Address bases: cell 65 holds ADD 69. To move the routine down by d cells, subtract d from every address in its
instructions that points into cells 27-98 and from the address bases; addresses 00 and 99 stay as they are.

